IF YOU REDISTRIBUTE
-------------------

MIT requires only that `LICENSE` travel with copies of the software, so this
file is not compelled by it. Carry it anyway. A copy that keeps `LICENSE` and
drops this one is formally compliant and has silently erased the boundary — and
`LICENSE` cannot point here, because appending a single line to it is what makes
GitHub stop recognising it as MIT.


WHY THE BOUNDARY
----------------

The structure is what is being given away. The words are not, because a CV and
a body of writing under a permissive software licence could be reposted
verbatim by anyone, including under someone else's name.

That is the same line `/architecture` draws when it invites replication: it
publishes the repositories, the decisions and the bar, not the author's record.
But the invitation on that page is an invitation — `LICENSE` and this file are
the licence.

*On the file layout:* GitHub classifies a repository's licence by matching the
text of `LICENSE` against known licences, and any text appended to the MIT
wording makes the match fail — the repository is then labelled "Other", which
tells a reader nothing about what they may do. Keeping `LICENSE` as the exact
MIT text and the boundary here makes the grant legible to a person and to a
machine at the same time.
